# Adversary — every environment variable the project reads.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Then fill in only what you need. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, so
# never put a real key here.
#
# Nothing below is required. With an empty file the whole corpus still runs:
# `pnpm demo` uses the scripted agent, the mock rail and SQLite, which is also
# exactly what CI does — no credentials, no network, no services.
#
# A variable already set in your shell always beats this file. Loading never
# overwrites something the environment already decided.


# ─── The model ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Only needed for `--agent ops` or `--agent naive`, which put a real language
# model under test instead of a fixed script. The default `--agent scripted`
# needs none of this.
#
# Set ONE of the three. They are checked in the order listed, so if you set more
# than one, the first wins — which is a confusing way to find out you have two.
#
# Costs real money. The corpus is 62 scenarios run twice; that is small, but it
# is not free.

# Google AI Studio → Get API key.  https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Anthropic Console → API keys.  https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# OpenAI Platform → API keys.  https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Overrides the per-provider default model. Defaults:
#   gemini     gemini-2.5-flash
#   anthropic  claude-sonnet-4-5
#   openai     gpt-4o
# ADVERSARY_MODEL=


# ─── Reproducibility ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A live model is not deterministic, even at temperature 0. A cassette records
# its responses so a run can be repeated exactly — and replaying one needs no
# API key at all, so a published result can be reproduced by someone who holds
# no account with the provider that produced it.
#
# Set both or neither. A path with no mode is refused rather than guessed at,
# because guessing wrong overwrites a recording.

# ADVERSARY_CASSETTE=.cassettes/ops-run.json
# ADVERSARY_CASSETTE_MODE=replay     # record | replay


# ─── Storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SQLite by default, in ./adversary.sqlite. Nothing to configure.

# ADVERSARY_DB_DIALECT=postgres
# ADVERSARY_PG_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/adversary
# ADVERSARY_SQLITE_PATH=./adversary.sqlite

# Turns on the Postgres half of the migration tests, which skip by default so
# `pnpm test` works with nothing running. CI sets this.
# ADVERSARY_TEST_PG_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/adversary


# ─── Corpus ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Where the scenario YAML lives. Change it to run your own corpus against the
# same harness.

# ADVERSARY_SCENARIOS=./scenarios


# ─── Deliberately absent ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There is no way to point this harness at a payment provider from
# configuration. The live rail refuses to construct on anything but a test-mode
# key, and it is not wired to the CLI at all. See docs/THREAT-MODEL.md.
#
# An earlier version of this file listed ADVERSARY_RAIL and three RAZORPAY_*
# placeholders. No code has ever read any of them. A sample file that lists
# variables nothing reads teaches people to set things that do nothing, and -
# worse here than usual - invites someone to put a payment credential in a file
# on the assumption that something will pick it up.
